{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794611147,"nonce":"Tiy5AyWxRWn077sNS21nS93b0m0=","title":"A CSS animation of opacity cannot ride rows: every bound opacity is an offscreen group","message":"Found while carrying CSS transform animations on rows (the issue for that is closed). The same animation with opacity added is left on the baked path, because on rows it is slower than that path. A bound opacity is pushed as a group, which the renderer draws in an offscreen layer, and each one costs about 0.3 ms. With 140 cards animating opacity in animation_paths.rs: 43 to 46 ms a frame on rows, against 10.9 ms baked without text and 47.8 ms painted in full with text.\n\nOpacity is the common case. All but one of cn's keyframes animate it (skeleton and progress pulses, tab, tooltip and popover enter and exit, fade in and out), so a page of skeleton blocks is what this would help most, and baking costs a texture per block (91 MB against 911 MB for 140 transform-animated cards in a window).\n\nWanted: a fade that needs no group where it is not needed, which is where the node's primitives do not overlap each other (a skeleton block is one primitive), by letting the row carry the opacity per primitive as it does for a node with no group. Whether a node qualifies has to be decided when it is painted. For nodes that do overlap (a card with a background and text) the group stays, or becomes a cheaper layer.\n\nRelated: 122c606, 54771c4 (P5).","files":null}]}